inary display of temper that's  not hearsay that's direct evidence from  somebody involved do you feel that your  language or actions were appropriate in  your as it's described in the report  your extraordinary position of power  forgive me sarah jane you're just  repeating an allegation with which i had  already dealt but i'm very happy to  address it yes a complainant claims that  i threw a phone when the witness who was  allegedly brought in to pick up the  pieces was asked about it that witness  did not repeat not corroborate the  allegation there was a second allegation  that i had thrown a phone and in that  respect the person with whom i'd been in  a meeting was asked for his testimony  and he said categorically in terms  unmistakable that i had not done so so i  stand by my point that the people who  were supposedly witnesses at the event  said no  this did not happen there were twenty  the decision makers have chosen to  conclude that it did  21 complaints no because i  thank you sarah sarah jen thank you very  much indeed for the question but the  answer is no and i'll tell you why  because i don't believe in faux  apologies this is a flawed process it's  chronically unjust it appertains to  matters up to 12 and a half years ago  from next month as i'm sure your viewers  will want to know  the bullying process the investigative  process will apply  only to matters reported that occurred  within 12 months okay so 12 months from  april and i'm being judged against 12  years the evidence is set up at that  time  we keep hearing that parliament doesn't  have an hr department it doesn't have  the due process for complaints to be  made  when that scheme was introduced they  then came forward but let me just put  this to you is there a chance that  perhaps you bullied people without  realizing because  a lot of the time bullies don't realize  their bullying  that is certainly the case but the fact  is no i don't accept that because there  are two criteria sarah 